        "explanation":  "MAOIs increase levels of biogenic amine neurotransmitters (serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine) by preventing their degradation. There are two types of MAO enzyme: MAO-A, which breaks down serotonin, norepinephrine, and dopamine; and MAO-B, which breaks down dopamine. It is contraindicated to give meperidine with an MAOI. Because these drugs increase intrasynaptic levels of biogenic amine neurotransmitters, they should not be given with other drugs that do the same. There have been reports of death in patients given MAOIs and meperidine simultaneously. Patients should inform each of the doctors that they are seeing that they are taking an MAOI. Lithium can be given with MAOIs. When switching a patient from an SSRI to an MAOI, you need to allow a 14-day washout (28 days for fluoxetine). This is because the combination of these drugs in a patient\u0027s system at the same time can potentiate a serotonin syndrome. Orthostatic hypotension is a major side effect of the MAOIs. Other side effects include weight gain, edema, sexual dysfunction, and insomnia. Moclobemide and the selegiline patch only weakly potentiate the pressor effects of tyramine, so often do not require a tyramine-free diet at low doses. Psychopharmacology K\u0026S Chapter 29",
