        "answerText":  "",
        "explanation":  "Botulinum toxin type A is FDA-approved for the treatment of cervical dystonia, blepharospasm, hemifacial spasm, strabismus, and axillary hyperhidrosis, and it\u0027s approved cosmetically for hyperactive glabellar lines of the procerus muscles of the forehead. Although not FDA-approved for spasticity related to multiple sclerosis, it is an excellent treatment for that particular disorder. Botulinum toxin type A is also used off-label for the treatment of migraine. The mechanism of action of botulinum toxin type A is through blockade of neuromuscular transmission via blockade of presynaptic acetylcholine release. Botulinum toxin type A has no place in the treatment of RLS. Neurology B\u0026D Chapter 48",
