        "isMultiSelect":  false,
        "answerText":  "",
        "explanation":  "Here is another chance to understand glycine if you did not master it after Question 13. Glycine is a neurotransmitter synthesized from serine. It is a necessary adjunctive neurotransmitter at the NMDA receptor that binds with glutamate. It is also an independent inhibitory neurotransmitter with its own receptors that open chloride ion channels. The activity of glycine on the NMDA receptor is an area of research for schizophrenia, with some studies showing improvement in negative symptoms with the use of glycine or glycine analogues. The highest concentrations of glycine receptors have been found in the spinal cord. Mutations of this receptor lead to a rare condition called hyperekplexia, of which the main symptom is an exaggerated startle response. Basic Neuroscience K\u0026S Chapter 1",
