        "answerText":  "",
        "explanation":  "Sleep terrors (non-REM sleep arousal disorder) are characterized by recurrent episodes of awakening and screaming during the first third of the night. The patient has intense fear, autonomic arousal, sweating, tachycardia, and rapid breathing. The patient is unresponsive to efforts of others to comfort him or her. There are no dreams recalled, and there is amnesia for the event. Small doses of diazepam are often useful to stop the episodes. Sleep-Wake Disorders K\u0026S Chapter 16",
