        "answerText":  "",
        "explanation":  "Cognitive behavioral therapy is founded on the principle that people make assumptions that affect their thoughts. Their thoughts then affect their mood. The goal of the therapy is therefore to uncover assumptions and thoughts that may be both faulty and automatic and determine how they contribute to changes in mood. Then the therapy aims to correct these faulty thoughts and stop them from being automatic. The other answer choices are all appropriate pieces of psychodynamic therapy and psychoanalysis. They are not, however, part of cognitive behavioral therapy. Psychotherapy K\u0026S Chapter 28",
