        "explanation":  "Answer choice B refers to Williams\u0027 syndrome. The syndrome is a result of an autosomal microdeletion on chromosome 7q11. Features of the disorder include cardiac valvular stenosis, hypotonia, hyperacusis, short stature and \"elfin\" facies, low nasal bridge, unusual ease with strangers and pleasant demeanor, developmental delay, and strong language skills. The disorder has no known cure and is strongly associated with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder. The distractors in this question are described in greater detail in other questions in this volume. Trisomy 21 is, of course, Down\u0027s syndrome. Deletion in chromosome 15q is Angelman\u0027s syndrome. Deletion of 45 XO refers to Turner\u0027s syndrome. Klinefelter\u0027s syndrome is a result of an extra sex chromosome, 47 XXY. Neurodevelopmental Disorders B\u0026D Chapter 7",
