        "isMultiSelect":  false,
        "answerText":  "",
        "explanation":  "In approximately 80% of patients with stiff-person syndrome, the disorder develops as a nonparaneoplastic phenomenon. It is often diagnosed by finding anti-GAD antibodies (glutamic acid decarboxylase) in the serum and CSF. Characteristic of stiff-person syndrome is fluctuating rigidity of the axial musculature with superimposed spasms. Muscle stiffness primarily affects the lower trunk and legs, but it can extend to the arms, shoulders, and neck. The paraneoplastic form of stiff-person syndrome is usually associated with breast and lung cancers and Hodgkin\u0027s disease. Treatment of the tumor and the use of corticosteroids may improve paraneoplastic stiff-person syndrome. Intravenous immunoglobulin is useful in patients with nonparaneoplastic stiff-person syndrome. Because of the muscle stiffness involved it can be misdiagnosed as catatonia or a dystonic reaction. GABA-enhancing agents, such as benzodiazepines, gabapentin, and baclofen, provide symptomatic relief. Neurology B\u0026D Chapter 52G",
