        "isMultiSelect":  false,
        "answerText":  "",
        "explanation":  "Most studies point to a prevalence of Alzheimer\u0027s disease of about 20% to 40% in the 85 years and over age range. Other statistics of note include the fact that 50% to 60% of patients that are demented have Alzheimer\u0027s disease. The second most common type of dementia is vascular dementia, which accounts for approximately 15% to 30% of all dementia cases. Alzheimer\u0027s disease is slightly more prevalent in women than in men throughout the life cycle, about 1.3 to 1. Vascular dementia is more prevalent in men than women and is most common in the 60 to 70 years age range. About 10% to 15% of patients have comorbid Alzheimer\u0027s disease and vascular dementia. Neurocognitive Disorders/Public Policy K\u0026S Chapter 21",
